T*
() Tj
T*
(- id: H-TA-01; name: Tier 1-3 Insufficiency; when: Assessment only measures attendance, activity, or) Tj
T*
(satisfaction; rule: Tiers 1-3 are INSUFFICIENT for evaluating learning. They measure presence and) Tj
T*
(opinion, not competence. An assessment at Tier 1-3 tells you nothing about whether learning occurred.) Tj
T*
(NEVER accept these as evidence of learning effectiveness.; severity: CRITICAL) Tj
T*
(- id: H-TA-02; name: Knowledge Ceiling; when: Assessment only measures recall or recognition \(Tier 4\);) Tj
T*
(rule: Tier 4 \(Knowledge\) is NECESSARY but not SUFFICIENT. Knowing ? doing. A quiz that tests recall of) Tj
T*
(facts or recognition of correct answers reaches Tier 4 maximum. To go higher, the assessment must require) Tj
T*
(APPLYING knowledge in realistic contexts.; severity: MANDATORY) Tj
T*
(- id: H-TA-03; name: Decision-Making Minimum; when: Evaluating any module's assessment; rule: Tier 5) Tj
T*
(\(Decision-Making Competence\) is the MINIMUM acceptable standard. At this tier, learners make correct) Tj
T*
(decisions in realistic scenarios. The scenario must include: realistic context, multiple valid options,) Tj
T*
(consequences for choices, and ambiguity that mirrors real-world conditions.; severity: MANDATORY) Tj
T*
(- id: H-TA-04; name: Task Performance Target; when: Designing assessment for skills that require doing;) Tj
T*
(rule: Tier 6 \(Task Competence\) is the TARGET for most skills. At this tier, the learner actually PERFORMS) Tj
T*
(the task ? writes the code, gives the presentation, diagnoses the problem, builds the thing. Talking) Tj
T*
(about doing it is Tier 4-5. Actually doing it is Tier 6.; severity: MANDATORY) Tj
T*
(- id: H-TA-05; name: Realistic Context Requirement; when: Designing Tier 5+ assessments; rule: IF) Tj
T*
(assessment uses sanitized/simplified scenarios ? Not realistic ? Does not test transfer. Real-world) Tj
T*
(problems have ambiguity, incomplete information, time pressure, and distractions. Assessments must) Tj
T*
(include enough of these to be valid.; severity: MANDATORY) Tj
T*
(- id: H-TA-06; name: Recognition vs Recall Distinction; when: Multiple choice questions are used; rule:) Tj
T*
(IF assessment only uses multiple choice ? Tier 4 maximum \(recognition\). Recognition \(picking the right) Tj
T*
(answer from options\) is easier than recall \(producing the answer\). For Tier 5+, learners must produce) Tj
T*
(answers, make decisions, or perform tasks ? not just select from options.; severity: MANDATORY) Tj
T*
(- id: H-TA-07; name: Low-Tier VETO; when: Curriculum has only Tier 1-4 assessments; rule: VETO:) Tj
T*
(Curriculum with only Tier 1-4 assessments ? BLOCK. A curriculum where the highest assessment is a) Tj
T*
(knowledge quiz cannot prove learning transfer. At minimum one Tier 5+ assessment per module is required.;) Tj
T*
(severity: VETO) Tj
T*
(- id: H-TA-08; name: Happy Sheet Rejection; when: Satisfaction surveys presented as learning evidence;) Tj
T*
(rule: Satisfaction surveys \('happy sheets', 'smile sheets'\) are Tier 3. Research shows near-zero) Tj
T*
(correlation between satisfaction and learning. A learner who rates training 5/5 may have learned nothing.) Tj
T*
(A learner who rates 2/5 \(because it was challenging\) may have learned the most. NEVER accept satisfaction) Tj
T*
(as learning evidence.; severity: CRITICAL) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
